Appendix A Comparison of astrophysical observations with the redshift marginalized BNS mass population in COMPAS

Although the BNS mass function is roughly redshift-independent in our models, its shape varies significantly depending on binary physics choices and initial conditions (see, for example, Fig. 1 in van Son et al. (2024) and the primary-secondary mass corner plot for simulation variations in Section 2: link). Here, we compare the mass function for the fiducial COMPAS setup with observed astrophysical neutron star mass measurements. We note that this fiducial population aligns with the galactic binary pulsar population inferred by Farrow et al. (2019) and with binary neutron star merger populations observed in GW events (Abbott et al., 2023).

In Fig. 3, we show neutron star mass measurements for 21 GAIA candidates (El-Badry et al., 2024) and three NICER observations: PSR J0030+0451 (Riley et al., 2019), PSR J0740+6620 (Riley et al., 2021), and PSR J0437-4715 (Choudhury et al., 2024). We also display parameter estimation contours of the two BNS events detected through GWs so far: GW170817 (Abbott et al., 2017) and GW190425 (Abbott et al., 2020), analyzed with the IMRPhenomPv2_NRTidal waveform model and low-spin prior. As shown in Fig. 3, the range of masses covered by these events are broadly consistent with the fiducial BNS mass spectrum in COMPAS.

## Appendix B Gaussian mixture model fitting of the true binary neutron star population

We draw 10^{5} samples of source-frame chirp mass (M_{c}), logarithm of mass ratio (\\log q), and redshift (z) from COMPAS population using a bounded kernel density estimator with the reflection technique (Gasser and Muller, 1979; Hall and Wehrly, 1991). The bounds are,

M_{c}=(-\\infty,\\infty),\\ \\log q=(-\\infty,0],\\ z=[0,\\infty)

While M_{c} should theoretically be bounded between [0,\\infty), in practice, no sample falls below 1M_{\\odot}. Therefore, treating M_{c} as unbounded is sufficient.

From these, we first split off 50%, of the samples data as the "train" set, and fit GMMs with varying numbers of components with them. Then we evaluate the likelihood of the rest 50% "test" set samples to optimize number of GMM components. We denote the normalized population fitted by the GMM as f_{\\rm 3D}^{\\rm GMM}(M_{c},q,z). This f_{\\rm 3D}^{\\rm GMM}(M_{c},q,z) is a representation of the true population generated by COMPAS.

See Fig. 4 and the accompanying material for the three-dimensional contours and their GMM fits. Animations showing how the mass distribution evolves with redshift can be found here.

## Appendix C Mock data generation

We summarize the mock data generation process for detector-frame chirp mass (M_{c}^{\\rm det}), mass-ratio (q), and luminosity distance (D_{L}) as follows:

1. COMPAS provides the number of merging BNSs per unit volume per unit merging time for discrete three-dimensional source-frame samples of masses and redshift. By multiplying the number of merging BNSs per unit volume per unit merging time by 4\\pi\\frac{dV_{c}}{dz}\\frac{T_{\\rm obs}}{(1+z)}, we obtain the expected number of BNS mergers, N_{\\rm exp}. Here we assume the Planck 2018 cosmology (Aghanim et al., 2020), with V_{c} and T_{\\rm obs} representing the comoving volume and observation time, respectively. We then perform a Poisson draw with a mean of N_{\\rm exp} to estimate the number of BNS mergers, N_{\\rm obs}, that would be observed within the specified observation time, assuming no selection effects, N_{\\rm obs}\\sim{\\rm Poisson}(N_{\\rm exp})

2. Given N_{\\rm obs}, we generate the true values of M_{c}^{t}, q^{t}, and z^{t} by resampling from the true population f_{\\rm 3D}^{\\rm GMM}(M_{c}^{t},q^{t},z^{t}): M_{c}^{t},\\ q^{t},\\ z^{t}\\sim f_{\\rm 3D}^{\\rm GMM}(M_{c}^{t},q^{t},z^{t}) We convert these to the detector-frame values by multiplying M_{c}^{t} by (1+z^{t}) and calculating the luminosity distance, D_{L}^{t} for z^{t} using Planck 2018 cosmology: M_{c}^{\\rm det,t}=M_{c}^{t}(1+z),\\ D_{L}^{t}=D_{L}^{t}(z^{t};\\ {\\rm Planck\\ 2018})

Here, the Planck 2018 cosmology assumes a flat \\LambdaCDM model with H_{0}=67.66 km/s/Mpc and \\Omega_{M}=0.31 (Aghanim et al., 2020). Other cosmological parameters are not relevant within the redshift range considered. We sample the Finn and Chernoff parameter (Finn and Chernoff, 1993), \\Theta^{t}, from a Beta(2,4) distribution, which approximates the actual distribution closely: \\Theta^{t}\\sim\\texttt{Beta}(2,4)

3. To obtain PE samples of M_{c}^{\\text{det},t}, q^{t}, and D_{L}^{t} for each BNS observation, we should create a 'true' waveform and inject it into the noise of an xG detector. The resulting signal with added noise provides us'mock' GW data, and we perform a full PE analysis on it. Given the computational expense and the lack of fully established techniques for PE in the next-generation multi-detector setup, we resort to an approximate PE approach (Fishbach et al., 2020; Fairhurst et al., 2023; Farah et al., 2023; Essick and Fishbach, 2023). We first calculate the true SNR, \\rho^{t}, using M_{c}^{\\text{det},t}, q^{t}, \\Theta^{t}, and D_{L}^{t} for each BNS merger: \\rho^{t}=\\left[4\\int_{0}^{\\infty}df\\frac{|h(M_{c}^{\\text{det},t},q^{t},\\Theta ^{t},D_{L}^{t};f)|^{2}}{S_{n}(f)}\\right]^{\\frac{1}{2}} (C1) Here, h(M_{c}^{\\text{det},t},q^{t},\\Theta^{t},D_{L}^{t};f) is the waveform model, and S_{n}(f) is the power spectral density of Cosmic Explorer with a 40 km baseline (Srivastava et al., 2022)1. We use the single-spin precessing waveform model IMRPhenomPv2(Husa et al., 2016; Khan et al., 2016) with all individual spin components set to zero. Evaluating the integral in equation (C1) is computationally intensive, especially across the parameter space of BNSs. Therefore, we pre-compute the integral for a two-dimensional grid of face-on masses at a reference distance D_{L}^{t}=1 Gpc and interpolate for the desired masses. We then find \\rho^{t} at our required Finn and Chernoff parameter and luminosity distance by scaling: Footnote 1: For the latest power spectral density data, refer to the URL: [https://dcc.cosmicexplorer.org/CE-T2000017/public](https://dcc.cosmicexplorer.org/CE-T2000017/public)

\\rho^{t}(M_{c}^{\\text{det},t},q^{t},\\Theta^{t},D_{L}^{t})=\\frac{\\Theta^{t}}{D _{L}^{t}}\\times\\rho^{t}(M_{c}^{\\text{det},t},q^{t},\\Theta^{t}=1,D_{L}^{t}=1\\ \\text{Gpc})

We determine the observed SNR, \\rho_{\\text{obs}}, by adding Gaussian noise with a standard deviation of 1 to \\rho^{t}:

\\rho^{\\text{obs}}\\sim\\text{N}(\\rho^{t},1) (C2)

Finally, we apply a selection cut by retaining only BNS mergers with \\rho^{\\text{obs}}>8.

4. For BNS mergers above the selection threshold, we aim to create and sample from the joint posterior distribution of the parameters: detector-frame chirp mass, logarithm of mass ratio, Finn and Chernoff parameter, and luminosity distance. The posterior distribution is given by: P(M_{c}^{\\text{det}},\\log q,\\Theta,D_{L}|M_{c}^{\\text{det,obs}},\\log q^{\\text {obs}},\\Theta^{\\text{obs}},D^{L,\\text{obs}})\\propto\\Big{|}\\frac{\\partial\\rho}{ \\partial D_{L}}\\Big{|}\\times\\pi(M_{c}^{\\text{det}},\\log q,\\Theta,\\rho)\\times \\mathcal{L}(M_{c}^{\\text{det,obs}},\\log q^{\\text{obs}},\\Theta^{ \\text{obs}},\\rho^{\\text{obs}}|M_{c}^{\\text{det}},\\log q,\\Theta,\\rho) (C3) where \\pi(M_{c}^{\\text{det}},\\log q,\\Theta,\\rho) represents the joint prior over the parameters, and M_{c}^{\\text{det,obs}},\\log q^{\\text{obs}},\\Theta^{\\text{obs}},\\rho^{\\text{ obs}} are the observed values of these parameters. To draw samples of M_{c}^{\\text{det}}, \\log q, \\Theta, and D_{L} from Equation (C3), we proceed as follows: 1. **Calculate Observed PE Uncertainty:** We determine the PE uncertainties by scaling the benchmark values from Vitale and Evans (2017) according to the observed SNR (\\rho^{\\text{obs}}). We denote the error bars on M_{c}^{\\text{det}},\\log q, and \\Theta as \\sigma_{M_{c}^{\\text{det}}},\\sigma_{\\log q}, and \\sigma_{\\Theta}, respectively.

2. **Draw Observed Quantities:** For each event, draw one sample of the observed quantities: M_{c}^{\\rm det,obs}\\sim{\\rm N}(M_{c}^{\\rm det},\\sigma_{M_{c}^{\\rm det}}),\\ \\log q^{\\rm obs}\\sim{\\rm N}_{(-\\infty,0]}(\\log q,\\sigma_{\\log q}),\\ \\Theta^{\\rm obs}\\sim{\\rm N}_{[0,0.25]}(\\Theta,\\sigma_{\\Theta}) (C4) where for {\\rm N}_{[a,b]}(\\mu,\\sigma) is a truncated normal distribution with mean \\mu and standard deviation \\sigma within the bounds [a,b]. One notable difference between our mock data generation process and that of Fishbach et al. (2020) is that while Fishbach et al. (2020) assumed M_{c}^{\\rm det} follows a lognormal distribution to avoid negative values of M_{c}^{\\rm det,obs}, we verified that for our values of \\sigma_{M_{c}^{\\rm det}}, M_{c}^{\\rm det,obs} is never negative.

3. **Generate Multiple Samples:** For each observed event, generate N_{\\rm samp} samples (ranging from 1000 to 8000, depending on the number of events): M_{c}^{\\rm det}\\sim{\\rm N}(M_{c}^{\\rm det,obs},\\sigma_{M_{c}^{\\rm det}}),\\ \\log q\\sim\\frac{{\\rm N}_{(-\\infty,0]}(\\log q^{\\rm obs},\\sigma_{\\log q})}{\\Phi \\big{(}\\frac{-\\log q}{\\sigma_{\\log q}}\\big{)}},\\ \\Theta\\sim\\frac{{\\rm N}_{[0,0.25]}(\\Theta^{\\rm obs},\\sigma_{\\Theta})}{\\Big{[}\\Phi \\big{(}\\frac{0.25-\\Theta}{\\sigma_{\\Theta}}\\big{)}-\\Phi\\big{(}-\\frac{\\Theta}{ \\sigma_{\\Theta}}\\big{)}\\Big{]}},\\ \\rho\\sim{\\rm N}(\\rho^{\\rm obs},1) (C5) \\Phi(.) represents the cumulative distribution for normal distribution.

4. **Calculate Luminosity Distance:** Using the samples of M_{c}^{\\rm det},\\log q,\\Theta, and \\rho from (C5), determine D_{L} for each event as: D_{L}=\\frac{\\Theta}{\\rho}\\times\\rho^{t}(M_{c}^{\\rm det},\\log q,\\Theta=1,D_{L}^{\\rm fid}=1{\\rm Gpc}) (C6) (e) **Reweight Samples:** Reweight the samples by: w=\\Big{|}\\frac{\\partial\\rho}{\\partial D_{L}}\\Big{|}=\\frac{\\rho}{D_{L}} (C7) Finally, for each of the N_{obs} BNS mergers, we obtain N_{\\rm samp} samples of M_{c}^{\\rm det}, \\log q, \\Theta, and D_{L} which together represent the posterior distribution of PE using a uniform PE prior.

## Appendix D Method

In Appendices B and C, we discussed generating mock data to represent PE samples in Cosmic Explorer for different true population distributions. Next, we estimate the cosmological parameters, \\vec{\\Omega}_{C}, using these datasets. To achieve this, we deconvolve the error bars induced by PE (Bovy et al., 2011). This process yields the rate-marginalized posterior distribution of \\vec{\\Omega}_{C}, incorporating a hierarchical prior proportional to the inverse of the rate (Essick and Farr, 2022).

The posterior distribution of \\vec{\\Omega}_{C} is approximately given by (Loredo, 2004; Mandel et al., 2019):

{\\rm Posterior\\ of\\ \\vec{\\Omega}_{C}\\ \\stackrel{{\\rm approx}}{{\\propto}}\\ \\pi(\\vec{\\Omega}_{C})\\xi^{-N_{\\rm obs}}\\prod_{i}^{N_{\\rm obs}}\\sum_{M_{c}^{\\rm det,i},q^{i},D_{L}^{i}} \\frac{1}{\\pi_{\\rm PE}(M_{c}^{\\rm det},q,D_{L})}.\\frac{\\frac{dz}{dD_{L}}}{1+z}.\\pi(M_{c},q,z)\\Bigg{|}_{M_{c}=\\frac{M_{c}^{\\rm det}}{1+z(D_{L},\\vec{\\Omega}_{C})},\\ z=z(D_{L},\\vec{\\Omega}_{C})} (D8)

Here, \\pi(\\vec{\\Omega}_{C}) represents the hierarchical prior for \\vec{\\Omega}_{C} and \\xi is the selection function corresponding to the SNR cut of 8 in Cosmic Explorer noise. M_{c}^{\\rm det,i}, q^{i}, and D_{L}^{i} are the samples from the i-th event's PE posterior. We also fully marginalize over the Finn and Chernoff parameter, \\Theta since its population is independent of masses and redshift and fixed by isotropy.

We calculate the selection function, \\xi, by generating injections in the detector frame and performing a Monte Carlo summation in the detector frame (Tiwari, 2018; Farr, 2019),

\\xi\\ (\\vec{\\Omega}_{C})\\approx\\frac{1}{N_{\\rm draw}}\\sum_{M_{c}^{\\rm det,inj},q ^{\\rm inj},D_{L}^{\\rm inj}}\\frac{1}{p_{\\rm draw}(M_{c}^{\\rm det},q,D_{L})}. \\frac{\\frac{dz}{dD_{L}}}{1+z}.\\pi(M_{c},q,z)\\Bigg{|}_{M_{c}=\\frac{M_{c}^{\\rm det}}{1+z(D_{L},\\vec{\\Omega}_{C})},\\ z=z(D_{L},\\vec{\\Omega}_{C})} (D9)

Here, p_{\\rm draw} is the distribution from which the injections are sampled, and M_{c}^{\\rm det,inj},q^{\\rm inj},D_{L}^{\\rm inj} are the injections that meet the detection threshold of the SNR cut of 8. In Equations (D8) and (D9), dz/dD_{L} and 1/(1+z) are Jacobiansto convert the population density in the source frame into a density over the samples in the detector frame. We also ensure that the effective number of samples is greater than four times the number of observed BNS mergers (Farr, 2019; Essick & Farr, 2022).

For the correlated case, we use the injected population f_{\\rm 3D}^{\\rm GMM}(M_{c},q,z) as described in Appendix B:

\\pi_{\\rm corr}(M_{c},q,z)=\\pi_{\\rm inj}(M_{c},q,z)=f_{\\rm 3D}^{\\rm GMM}(M_{c},q,z) (D10) where \\pi_{\\rm corr}(M_{c},q,z) and \\pi_{\\rm inj}(M_{c},q,z) represent the correlated and injected populations, respectively.

For the uncorrelated case in Fig. 5, we model the mass population, marginalized over redshift, using a two-dimensional Gaussian Mixture Model, f_{\\rm 2D}^{\\rm GMM}(M_{c},q), and the redshift population, marginalized over masses, using a one-dimensional Gaussian Mixture Model, f_{\\rm 1D}^{\\rm GMM}(z). The uncorrelated population is then given by:

\\pi_{\\rm uncorr}(M_{c},q,z)=\\Big{(}\\int_{\\forall z}dz\\ \\pi_{\\rm inj}(M_{c},q,z) \\Big{)}\\Big{(}\\int_{\\forall M_{c},q}dM_{c}\\ dq\\ \\pi_{\\rm inj}(M_{c},q,z)\\Big{)}=f_{ \\rm 2D}^{\\rm GMM}(M_{c},q)f_{\\rm 1D}^{\\rm GMM}(z) (D11)

Here, \\pi_{\\rm uncorr}(M_{c},q,z) represents the uncorrelated mass-redshift population. The population drops to zero beyond mass ratio 1 and below redshift 0. The GMM underpredicts the probability density near these hard cutoffs. To compensate for this, we use the reflection technique (Gasser & Muller, 1979; Hall & Wehrly, 1991).

To model the distance (D_{L})-redshift (z) relationship, we adopt a flat \\LambdaCDM cosmology with a radiation density parameter set to zero:

D_{L}=\\frac{c}{H_{0}}(1+z)\\int_{0}^{z}dz^{\\prime}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\Omega_{M}(1+z^{ \\prime})^{3}+(1-\\Omega_{M})}} (D12)

We assume uniform priors for the dimensionless Hubble constant (H_{0}/100 km s{}^{-1}Mpc{}^{-1}), ranging from 0.2 to 1.5, and for the matter density parameter (\\Omega_{M}), ranging from 0 to 1. These priors are applied to both evolving and non-evolving mass models. We use the No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS) in combination with NumPyro(Phan et al., 2019; Bingham et al., 2019) and JAX(Bradbury et al., 2018) for sampling the cosmological parameters. JAX enables compatibility with automatic differentiation, facilitating efficient sampling.